e having a rare  disorder is not rare at all  in fact the truth is that rare disorders  are quite common  in this presentation i will be talking  about a certain class of methods that  can be used to understand rare disorders  specifically methods for predicting  disease genes  i'll begin by talking about  the impact of rare disease and why we  should care about developing methods for  the rare disease community despite the  small number of individuals with any  given disorder  i will also discuss some of the biggest  challenges in rare disease research  i will then talk about gene prediction  and how it can be used to investigate  rare disorders  finally i will review the prior work to  develop methods in this area  and i will highlight opportunities for  the development of new methods  so what is the impact of rare disorders  in the united states the orphan drug act  of 1983 defined a rare disorder as one  that affects fewer than 200 000 people  in the u.s  based on this definition there are about  seven thousand disorders that are  considered rare  within the united states an estimated 25  to 30 million people have a rare  disorder  that's about one out of every 10 people  based on the eu's definition of a rare  disorder as one that is life-threatening  or chronically debilitating and that  affects fewer than one out of every two  thousand people the global prevalence  was estimated in 2019 to be between 263  and 446 million people or about three  and a half to six percent of the world's  population  an estimated 70 of rare disorders begin  before birth or during childhood  and another 18 percent can be uh can  begin in either children or adults  this means that most rare disorders are  lifelong ordeals and in many cases are  fatal early in life  rare disorders affect people's lives in  more ways than simply the physical  manifestations  a survey of over 1 000 adults living  with a rare disorder revealed that  individuals with rare diseases tend to  have worse quality of life outcomes than  not only the general population but also  those living with chronic conditions  that are more common  so why would that be the case  another study suggested that the very  fact that the disorder was rare  introduced additional challenges  especially due to the lack of knowledge  about the disorder by health care  professionals  due to the dearth of research on rare  disorders the so-called diagnostic  odyssey that many rare disease patients  go through can last many years  even if a correct diagnosis is  determined there's often little  information that can be offered about  the mechanism of the disease let alone  any treatment  in my case although no one knew what had  caused my condition i was fortunate that  there were some treatments available to  try to reduce my contractures and build  up some muscle  throughout the early years of my  childhood my treatments consisted of  physical and occupational therapy  orthotic devices such as splints casts  and braces  and at least six surgeries  through these treatments i did gradually  gain some strength and flexibility  however even with these early victories  the effectiveness of these methods was  still quite limited  at the age of three i was given perhaps  my most important prescription  a license to drive  yes my first hot rod was pink  i have lived my life unable to walk or  even stand and while i am lucky to be  mostly independent throughout the day i  am still fully reliant on assistance for  various activities of daily living such  as simply getting in and out of bed  currently there are no treatments that  directly target the cellular and  molecular changes that produced my  condition the startling reality is that  over 90  of rare disorders do not have an fda  approved treatment  but this doesn't have to be the case  with computational methods that can  address the challenges of rare disorders  we can begin to understand the  biological processes behind them and  start to bring down the number of  disorders without treatment  so now that we have a better picture of  the impact of rare disorders  let's discuss some of the biggest  challenges in researching rare disease  the main challenge facing research on  individual rare disorders is limited  data  when studying a common disorder a  typical computational design might look  something like this  a researcher collects some kind of data  type from the patients in a large study  such as one or maybe a combination of  these omics data types  this data is then associated with the  patient's phenotype data or other  metadata to train and validate a model  that predicts the phenotype or other  patient outcome  the model created can then be used  either for prediction of unknown  outcomes in new patients  or as a way to infer which variables  such as genes are most likely involved  in producing the observed outcomes  however this type of study may require  hundreds to thousands of patients  in reality the challenge of limited data  is not specific to rare disease so the  methods i will talk about can be useful  for more common conditions as well but  rare disorders do represent an extreme  challenge in this regard as their much  sparser patient populations make  recruitment and data collection a much  more challenging and prolonged process  in addition to the issue of limited data  there are also biological challenges  that must be considered  rare disorders may sometimes seem  relatively simple  it has been estimated that 80 percent of  rare disorders are genetic in origin  and these are mostly believed to be  mendelian meaning they are caused by  just one gene  so a fair question might be  why don't we just treat each disorder  individually by editing the mutations in  each of these genes  in the long run this may be something  that needs to happen at least for some  disorders  however this strategy will not be enough  in addition to this approach currently  being intractable  there are also a number of other issues  that will necessitate a different  strategy  one that is focused not on any  individual gene but on the actual  disease process itself  to talk about the biological challenges  of rare disorders i will use  arthrogryposis multiplex congenita or  what i will simply call arthrogryposis  as a case study  understanding these challenges will be  important for understanding how gene  prediction methods can provide new  insights into rare diseases  when i told my story i mentioned that i  had been labeled as having  arthrogryposis multiplex congenita but  that this was not an actual diagnosis  this is because arthrogryposis is not a  single disorder  rather it is the medical term used to  describe the presence of contractures or  stiff joints  that are present at birth in multiple  areas of the body  in other words it is a phenotype  it turns out that this phenotype is a  characteristic of over 400 different  disorders  so far mutations in more than 400 genes  have been associated with arthrogryposis  and like most other rare diseases many  of the disorders presenting with  arthrogryposis are believed to be caused  by just a single gene  but let's discuss some of the biological  complexities that must be considered  the first complexity is that for  arthrogryposis as for many rare  disorders a causative mutated gene is  not the whole story  the current consensus is that the  contractures develop in two phases  in the first phase a mutation causes  fetal  fetal akinesia or reduced fetal movement  in the second phase it is actually not  the initial mutation but the reduction  in movement that has been shown to  produce contractures through changes to  the tissues in in and around the joint  this means that while repairing the  mutated gene may be important it will  likely not be enough to restore normal  function once the contractures have  already formed  the second challenge is that  arthrogryposis is not always caused by a  mutated gene  in fact the 