 let's first view these methods  through the lens of how much we know  about the genes that cause a disorder of  interest  to understand this perspective let's  revisit this figure showing the more  traditional way of investigating a  disorder  again for a rare disease we will likely  not have a large cohort of patients  if there is any any omics data from any  patients with the disorder it will  likely be of just one data type like  whole exome sequencing or maybe rna-seq  so we likely won't be working with a  very large data set  so let's remove these from the picture  now i haven't talked about sources of  prior knowledge yet and those will  certainly come into play but to keep  things simple for now let's also remove  them from the picture  so what can we use as inputs to our  model  well that depends on how much we already  know about the genes involved in the  condition we're investigating  let's first look at the worst case  scenario where we have no information  about any genes associated with the  disorder  here the goal is to just find any genes  that may be linked with this disease  in this scenario the input to the model  could simply be keywords or ontology  terms such as from the human phenotype  ontology or the gene ontology that  pertain to the disorders phenotypes or  biological processes  there are multiple approaches that have  been developed for this problem but they  all involve predicting genes for one  disorder based on its similarity to the  phenotypes of other disorders  these include text mining approaches as  well as methods that use disease gene  association networks and machine  learning  for other disorders we may have  information about genes whose expression  is correlated with the disease phenotype  this might look similar to the scenario  for more common disorders where omics  data is correlated to patients  phenotypes  again though with rare disorders we  likely don't have the patient numbers or  the multiple data types to be able 