ers  in fact many disease gene prediction  methods incorporate a disease similarity  network and are therefore able to accept  either c genes or a diseased term as we  saw before  from which they can in firsy genes from  the disease network  because of this overlap in methods i  will highlight only a few examples when  i talk about using keywords to predict  disease genes  so let's talk about some of the methods  that have been developed  again if we don't know any genes  associated with our disorder of interest  we can use methods that make predictions  based on keywords or ontology terms  some of these methods mine the  literature for associations between  phenotypes and genes  in most cases  these relationships are identified by  statistically assessing their  co-occurrence in published abstracts and  papers  for instance a method called beagle  accepts any keywords such as phenotypes  associated with a disorder and searches  for genes that are mentioned with any of  those keywords in medline abstracts  it assesses the strength of the keyword  gene association by calculating the  jaccard similarity  which compares the number of abstracts  in which a keyword and gene appear  together to the number of abstracts  containing just a keyword or just the  gene  while text mining methods are limited to  genes already documented in the  literature  they may reveal new gene disease  associations that can be the starting  point for further investigation  the other methods that accept keywords  are based on the modular nature of genes  which is the observation that genes that  work toward a common function and  produce a common phenotype  often work together  by looking at disorders that are similar  to the one we are investigating we may  be able to identify genes that are  implicated in our disorder  typically this is done using  network-based methods  for example a method called prince  calculates a score for each disorder in  the omim database based on how similar  its mesh terms are to the disorder of  interest  so for instance if our disorder has a  phenotype of arthrogryposis then another  disorder with a phenotype of muscle  weakness would get a higher similarity  score than a disorder characterized by  osteoarthritis  since arthrogryposis and muscle weakness  are more closely related in the mesh  ontology  these disease similarity scores are then  used as seed values for the associated  disease genes  in a protein-protein interaction network  and prince propagates this information  through the network using a diffusion  algorithm  more recently graph learning methods  have been used to try to learn the most  meaningful relationships in the data  for example a method called prejaphene  embeds a heterogeneous network of  various biological data types  into a lower dimensional representation  to try to extract the most meaningful  representation of the data  it then uses these refined relationships  to construct a new dual layer disease  gene network which it uses to predict  the disease genes  now let's look at a few examples of  methods designed to use expression data  that correlates with the disease state  with these methods we are looking for  groups of genes whose coexpression  patterns  change in the disease samples versus the  control samples  by far the most widely used module  detection method is wgcna  which uses  hierarchical clustering to identify  modules of coexpressed genes across  samples  however while this method may work well  for some applications it may not be the  most effective for zeroing zeroing in on  groups of genes that are driving a  disease process  this is because it identifies  co-expression modules across all samples  before determining their level of  association with the disorder  in contrast while diff coax uses the  same hierarchical clustering process as  wgcna  it first takes into consideration the  changes in coexpression between disease  samples and control samples before  identifying modules  therefore the modules represent sets of  genes that are differentially  co-expressed between cases and controls  and therefore likely to be more directly  related to the actual disease process  in addition these methods can be  improved by incorporating additional  information  for example wu and stein used a  protein-functional interaction network  as evidence of true associations  and they use co-expression data to  weight to the edges of this network  they then perform markov clustering on  this weighted network to identify  coexpression modules  next we'll look at examples of methods  that can use known disease-associated  genes referred to as c genes to inform  their predictions  because c genes are typically directly  related to the mechanism of the disorder  or phenotype the use of seed genes  produces the highest confidence  predictions of genes that are also  involved in the disease process  therefore i will spend more time on this  category  the first class of methods i will talk  about are similarity profiling methods  also known as data fusion or ensemble  methods  genes that perform similar functions or  participate together in a pathway will  often share similar sequences have  overlapping functional annotations be  co-expressed or demonstrate similarity  in other ways  similarity profiling methods use these  similarity characteristics  to statistically determine which  candidate genes have the highest  resemblance to the seed genes that are  input into the model  one of the most commonly used similarity  profiling methods called endeavor  gathers different types of information  about the seed genes by accessing 75  different data sources providing  insights on such characteristics as  function pathway expression patterns  sequence and chemical interactions among  others  it then compares each candidate gene to  the seed genes and calculates a final  aggregated score of similarity  these methods make an assumption that  the various pieces of evidence should  all have an additive effect on a  gene's disease probability  therefore to get around this limitation  machine learning models have been  developed to learn the contributions of  the different evidence sources in  predicting disease genes  for example p brit extracts information  about the genes from various sources and  uses this information along with the  along with the seed genes to train a  bayesian ridge regression model  the great thing about similarity  profiling methods is that they can  easily take an unlimited number of data  sources into account providing a wide  range of evidence for possible  relationships between seed and candidate  genes  however these methods do not explicitly  model gene's multiple interactions with  one another or consider the complex  association patterns between genes and  other associated data  therefore networks have become a popular  structure used to model the actual  physical interactions and functional  relationships between genes  as well as their associations to other  entities  network-based methods are based on the  principle of guilt by association  again referring to the observation that  genes operate within functional modules  early methods were limited to looking at  the local environment around seed genes  for example  diamond works by first identifying the  direct neighbors of the seed genes and  determining which one is most highly  enriched for c gene connections  that gene is then labeled as another c  gene and the process is  repeated although the process expands  across the full network only direct  neighbors are considered at each  iteration  this assumption that disease genes are  directly connected to the seed genes  introduces the possibility of false  positives and also risks missing genes  that are important to the disease  process  in response to these limitations kohler  at all introduced what would become very  popular methods the random walk with  restart and the diffusion kernel method  these network propagation approaches  measure the strength of the relationship  between two genes based on the  probability of encountering one gene  when traversing the network starting at  the other gene  these methods can also be thought of as  measuring the flow of information  through the network starting at a given  gene  an example from the original random walk  paper demonstrates that two genes  separated by another can be scored  differently based on the connectedness  of the gene between them  or based on the number of paths  connecting the two genes  therefore network propagation methods  consider more global topological aspects  than methods based on local  relationships  when i talked about similarity profiling  methods i said that their advantage was  the ability to incorporate many sources  of information about the genes  omics data is inherently noisy  and if we limit our network to just high  confidence curated protein protein  interactions we will likely be missing a  lot of relevant information due to our  incomplete knowledge of these  interaction networks  in addition each individual data source  contains its own biases  because certain genes disorders or other  entities will be more well researched  than others  so an important developments in the  field of disease gene prediction was the  extension of network propagation  algorithms to knowledge graphs  which incorporate information from  multiple data sources  we have seen a couple examples of  heterogeneous knowledge graphs that  incorporate protein-protein interactions  with disease similarity networks  in 2019 valdeo olivas at all went even  further by extending the random walk  with restart algorithm to multiplex  heterogeneous networks  they demonstrated their algorithm by  using it to predict disease genes from a  network that  that connects a disease similarity  network  with three layers of different types of  gene information  global approaches using these various  network propagation methods have become  widely used but are they truly better  than the more local approaches using  direct neighbors or shortest paths  to answer this question shim at all  tested both a local strategy and a  global strategy  and demonstrated that actually neither  of these approaches consistently  outperformed the other in predicting the  top disease genes  instead they show that the performance  of a given approach depended on the  level of connectedness between the  actual disease genes  the method  that focused on local properties was  better at prediction for disorders with  highly connected genes whereas the  method focusing on global properties was  better at prediction for disorders with  genes that were more dispersed  throughout a pathway  for some disorders the implicated genes  are partially connected in a pathway and  may require a combination strategy  more generally we don't know what the  true patterns of connection are between  many of the genes which prevents us from  choosing the most optimum strategy  to try to get the best of both of these  approaches machine learning methods can  be used  with machine learning both local and  global topological features can be  selected as input to a model and the  model can be trained to weight these  features appropriately to obtain the  best prediction  for example ramadan it's all developed a  random forest model  using 13 topological features from their  network some of them local like degree  and shortest path measurements  and some of them global like random walk  measurements  one challenge that the task of disease  gene prediction poses to traditional  machine learning models  is the fact that only positive training  samples referring to the c genes are  known  whereas the class label for the vast  majority of training samples the  candidate genes  is unknown  therefore the model has no definite  examples of non-disease genes from which  to learn as a result a special class of  machine learning methods that uses  strategies for positive unlabeled  learning  have been applied to this problem as  well  for example catapult uses a biased  support vector machine to predict  disease genes based on random walks of  different lengths  since most of the candidate genes are  likely not involved in the disease  catapult trains the model by selecting a  random subset of candidate genes to  serve as negative examples  the support vector machine is biased  because it penalizes false negatives  more more heavily than false positives  since the examples of true positives are  actually known  as an alternative to hand selecting the  features to be used in a model there now  exist methods that can automatically  learn to represent a network structure  as a set of feature vectors  these vectors can then be used for  downstream analyses including as  features in a machine learning model  these graph learning methods include  network embedding and matrix  factorization  as well as deep learning which i will  discuss in the next slide  earlier we saw a method that used  network embedding to learn meaningful  relationships in the data to construct a  more refined network  another example of the use of network  embedding is smudge which uses a random  walk-based embedding model called  skipgram to represent each node's  environments as a vector  these vectors are then used as the  features in a neural network to predict  disease genes  while network embedding methods may  perform very well in certain cases the  embedding process is unsupervised or  semi-supervised so the topological  features that are extracted may or may  not be the most relevant features for  predicting the genes responsible for a  particular disorder  that's where deep learning comes in  because deep learning uses a supervised  strategy to transform the data as well  as  predictions  it actually learns the features that are  most relevant for predicting disease  genes  whether these are topological features  of 